## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end, scalable data ingestion with evolving schemas and strict access controls.

## Key Concepts
- Schema evolution and registry in GCS
- Event-driven ingest via Pub/Sub and Dataflow
- Idempotent writes to BigQuery and quarantined handling
- Schema drift detection and monitoring
- IAM minimal privileges and secure service-to-service communication

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator: fetch schema from registry, validate event, route accordingly
async function ingest(event) {
  const schema = await fetchSchema(event.tenant);
  if (validate(event, schema)) {
    writeBigQuery(event);
  } else {
    routeQuarantine(event);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement schema registry versioning and backward-compatibility rules?
- How would you test idempotence and drift detection at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of event-driven orchestration, idempotency, and minimal IAM in a data enrichment flow that uses Cloud Functions, Pub/Sub, and Cloud Run.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven pipeline with Cloud Functions, Pub/Sub, Cloud Run
- Idempotent writes via insertId and dedup strategies
- Dead-letter handling and exponential backoff retries
- Least privilege IAM and secure service-to-service communication

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Cloud Function: validate and publish to Pub/Sub
const {PubSub} = require('@google-cloud/pubsub');
const pubsub = new PubSub();
exports.validateAndPublish = (req, res) => {
  const evt = req.body; // simple schema check omitted
  const data = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(evt));
  pubsub.topic('enrich-events').publish(data);
  res.status(200).send('ok');
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test handling of out-of-order message delivery?
- What changes ensure exactly-once processing when writing to BigQuery?

